\begin_layout Title
Numerical calculations of two-dimensional generalized Brillouin zone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Author
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
As the non-Hermitian generalization of the Brillouin zone,
 the generalized Brillouin zone (GBZ) plays a central role in depicting the energy spectrum,
 skin localization,
 and band topology of non-Hermitian periodic lattices.
 While one-dimensional GBZ can be numerically calculated by the auxiliary GBZ method,
 its higher-dimensional generalization,
 the amoebic GBZ and the strip GBZ,
 are hard to compute due to their complicated algebraic structure.
 In this work,
 we propose an universal numerical algorithm for both the two types of GBZs by converting the algebraic geometry problem into polynomial root solving.
 We build a python package named `bfGBZ2py' for generic two-dimensional tight-binding models using brute-force root solving of univariate polynomials.
 A mesh generator is also integrated in the package to extract the topology of the GBZs,
 serving as a base space for topological invariant calculations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Non-Bloch band theory and 2D GBZ
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section,
 we give a brief introduction to the non-Bloch band theory,
 and two frameworks that generalize it to two and higher dimensions:
 the amoeba formulation and the strip-GBZ formulation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For non-Hermitian periodic lattices,
 under the periodic boundary condition (PBC),
 the lattice is described by the Bloch Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\mathbf{k})$
\end_inset

,
 whose eigenvalues over the Brillouin zone (BZ) form the PBC spectrum 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\mathrm{PBC}}$
\end_inset

.
 Under the open boundary condition (OBC),
 the non-Hermitian skin effect can drive the eigenstates away from Bloch waves,
 and the OBC spectrum 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\mathrm{OBC}}$
\end_inset

 in the thermodynamic limit generally deviates from 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\mathrm{PBC}}$
\end_inset

 [?lee2016,
 yaoWang2018].
 However,
 since the translation symmetry still holds,
 the Hamiltonian should share common eigenstates with the translation operators,
 resulting in good quantum number 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Facing this contradiction,
 the non-Bloch band theory recovers 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\text{OBC}}$
\end_inset

 by allowing complex momenta:
 writing 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}\equiv\rme^{\rmi k_{j}}$
\end_inset

 and analytically continuing the momenta into the complex plane,
 one obtains the non-Bloch Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\boldsymbol{\beta})$
\end_inset

.
 For an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-dimensional (
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

D) 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

-band tight-binding model,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\boldsymbol{\beta})$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $q\times q$
\end_inset

 matrix whose entries are Laurent polynomials in 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\beta}\equiv(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{n})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 extending the momenta to complex space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\end_inset

 doubles the dimensionality of the momentum space.
 Since the physical momentum space should fall back to the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

D Brillouin zone (BZ) as the non-Hermitian part tends to zero,
 the complex momentum space should have the same dimension as the BZ,
 which requires 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 real-valued constraints.
 By imposing these real-valued constraint on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{C}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

D subspace is obtained as the non-Hermitian generalization of the BZ,
 dubbed the generalized Brillouin zone (GBZ).
 For one-dimensional lattices,
 we assume the characteristic polynomial has the form,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\det\left[E-h(\beta)\right]=\sum_{j=-M}^{N}a_{j}\left(E\right)\beta^{j},\label{eq:char-poly-1d}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{j}(E)$
\end_inset

 are polynomials in 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

.
 Given a reference energy 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M+N$
\end_inset

 solutions of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 are obtained from the eigenvalue equation.
 The 1D GBZ constraint is proved to be,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left|\beta^{(M)}\left(E\right)\right|=\left|\beta^{(M+1)}(E)\right|,\label{eq:1D-GBZ-constraint}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{(j)}(E)$
\end_inset

 is the solution of eigenvalue equation ordered by 
\begin_inset Formula $|\beta^{(j)}(E)|\leq|\beta^{(k)}(E)|,\forall j<k$
\end_inset

.
 In general,
 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

) equals the number of OBC equations at the left (right) boundary.
 Hence,
 the physical picture of Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:1D-GBZ-constraint"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is that the exponential scaling of the two 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

middle
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 states is required to be the same,
 such that they are able to cancel each other at both boundaries simultaneously.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For numerical methods,
 the 1D GBZ of a tight-binding model can be computed via the auxiliary GBZ method (ref.
 ?).
 When Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:1D-GBZ-constraint"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) holds,
 there must exist 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi\in[0,2\pi)$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{(M+1)}=\beta^{(M)}\rme^{\rmi\phi}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 the GBZ is a subset of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

auxiliary GBZ
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is defined as the solution set of the equations,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\det\left[E-h(\beta)\right]=0,\\
\det\left[E-h(\beta\rme^{\rmi\phi})\right]=0,
\end{cases}\label{eq:aGBZ-eq}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

as 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 traverses 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,2\pi)$
\end_inset

.
 For tight-binding models,
 the degrees of 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 in the characteristic polynomial are all finite.
 Hence,
 a fixed value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 yields a finite solution set of 
\begin_inset Formula $(E,\beta)$
\end_inset

,
 which can be numerically computed by algebraic root-solving algorithms such as the resultant method or the homotopy continuation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast to the well-established framework in 1D lattices,
 the OBC energy spectrum and eigenstates exhibit complicated structure in two and higher dimensions.
 Due to the geometry-dependent skin effect (ref.
 ?),
 different geometries of the bulk yield different OBC spectra and eigenstates even when the system size is sufficiently large.
 Hence,
 the momentum-space Hamiltonian 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\beta)$
\end_inset

 is not sufficient to determine the non-Hermitian band structure.
 It is still an open question to give a 2D GBZ for an arbitrary geometry.
 Recently,
 there are two main frameworks that generally builds the higher dimensional GBZs.
 One is the amoeba formulation,
 which builds the GBZ by an algebraic concept named 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

amoeba
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The other is the strip-GBZ formulation,
 which is derived by sequential extension of a lattice along several axes.
 
\end_layout

