\begin_layout Standard
For numerical calculations,
 however,
 both the amoebic GBZ and the strip GBZ in 2D are much more complicated compared to the 1D GBZ.
 The main obstacle is that the computation of both types of 2D GBZs cannot be reduced to a root-solving problem,
 like the auxiliary GBZ approach for 1D GBZ.
 Hence,
 we adopt a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

brute force
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 method,
 which yields the 2D GBZs by sweeping all possible complex energies,
 and collecting the subsets of GBZ at each energy point.
 However,
 direct computation of Eq.
 (?) or Eq.
 (?) is still time consuming.
 When the data points are near the zeros of characteristic polynomial,
 the integrand of the both integrals diverges,
 resulting in slow convergence.
 To overcome this obstacle,
 we utilize the relation between the winding number and the roots,
 and convert the integration to a root-solving problem.
 In following sections,
 we will discuss this method in details.
\end_layout

